# EXPERIMENTAL AND CURRENTLY DISABLED. Reading this list is not enough to make
# the reduced-operator build work; it is recorded here with what was learned so
# the next attempt does not start from zero.
#
# ONNX Runtime can be compiled with only the operators a fixed set of models
# needs (`--include_ops_by_config`). For our three PP-OCRv5 graphs that is worth
# about 7.6 MB of the shipped .so. It was enabled by default in #32, shipped in
# #32's merge, and REVERTED here because the app cannot scan with it: session
# init dies with
#
#     Could not find an implementation for <Op>(<n>) node with name '...'
#
# WHY THIS LIST IS HARD TO GET RIGHT
#
# The operators a session needs are not the operators in the model file. ORT
# runs graph optimizations at session init (we set no level, so ORT_ENABLE_ALL)
# and those optimizations both CREATE and REMOVE operators:
#
#   - tools/python/create_reduced_build_config.py reads the raw .onnx, so it
#     misses everything the optimizer creates -- `com.microsoft.FusedConv`,
#     `FusedMatMul`, `SkipLayerNormalization`. That was the first failure.
#   - onnxruntime.tools.convert_onnx_models_to_ort optimizes first and derives
#     the list from the result, which fixes those -- and then the app failed on
#     `Pow(13)`, an operator the offline conversion had folded away but the
#     runtime optimizer kept. So the offline-optimized graph is not the same
#     graph the runtime produces either.
#
# Neither tool describes the session we actually run. The list below is the
# convert_onnx_models_to_ort output (which got furthest) and is known to be
# missing at least Pow.
#
# WHAT A REAL FIX PROBABLY LOOKS LIKE, in rough order of confidence:
#
#   1. Ship .ort-format models instead of .onnx. This is the flow ORT actually
#      supports for reduced builds: optimization happens ahead of time, so the
#      operator set is fixed and knowable. Costs a model-format change that also
#      touches device_sim and desktop, which load .onnx.
#   2. Pin the session's graph optimization level to match whatever level the
#      config was generated at, so runtime cannot invent an operator the list
#      does not have.
#   3. Union the raw-model and optimized-model lists. Cheap, strictly safer than
#      either alone, but still not a proof of completeness -- and the failure it
#      does not prevent is a crash on a user's phone.
#
# WHATEVER IS TRIED, THE GATE IS RUNNING IT:
#
#     ./scripts/android-e2e.sh "$(./scripts/e2e-fixtures.sh)" --pilot
#
# against a real arm64 device or an arm64 AVD, which an Apple Silicon Mac runs
# natively. No host build can catch this -- the host links pyke's stock
# prebuilt, which has every operator -- and no GitHub-hosted runner can either.
# Symbol inspection cannot catch it: reduction removes kernel *registrations*,
# not class definitions, so a broken build still shows ~50 FusedConv symbols.
#
# To regenerate the list (needs the python onnxruntime pinned to the SAME
# version build-ort-android.sh builds, since which fusions fire is a property of
# that version -- read it from ort-sys's build/download/dist.txt, the `ms@<ver>`):
#
#   python3 -m venv /tmp/ortvenv
#   /tmp/ortvenv/bin/pip install onnx flatbuffers 'onnxruntime==1.24.2'
#   mkdir -p /tmp/ortconv && cp models/*.onnx /tmp/ortconv/
#   /tmp/ortvenv/bin/python -m onnxruntime.tools.convert_onnx_models_to_ort \
#       /tmp/ortconv --optimization_style Fixed
#   # /tmp/ortconv/required_operators.config, keeping this header
#
# Models this list was generated from (shasum -a 256 models/*.onnx):
#   8aad3208eac0bda67cb68a4c03cf376f25f9638764b6aa115a2bfa1815e76600  PP-LCNet_x0_25_textline_ori.onnx
#   d5de5df358366210d16419b9636a2fc1efa5d7a20688f38a7869ec7b1a4f4f7d  PP-OCRv5_mobile_det.onnx
#   20b6945179e9aa1dabe8770a0e73dba9a05350431549e58d7e88837f033c2890  PP-OCRv5_mobile_rec.onnx
#
# The textline-orientation hash changed with core v0.10.0 (ocr-models-v1 ->
# ocr-models-v2): PP-LCNet_x1_0 -> PP-LCNet_x0_25. The OPERATOR LIST BELOW IS
# UNCHANGED and was deliberately not regenerated -- the converted x0_25 graph has
# the same 15 op types in the same counts as x1_0 (measured in beanbeaver-core,
# see its docs/ocr-models.md "Why x0_25 and not x1_0"). Only the recorded hashes
# moved, so the guard keeps matching the models it is supposed to describe. det
# and rec are byte-identical across the two releases, which is why their hashes
# are untouched.
#
ai.onnx;1;GlobalAveragePool,LayerNormalization,SimplifiedLayerNormalization
ai.onnx;6;HardSigmoid
ai.onnx;11;AveragePool,Conv,ConvTranspose
ai.onnx;13;Concat,Gemm,MatMul,ReduceMean,Resize,Shape,Sigmoid,Slice,Softmax,Split,Squeeze,Transpose,Unsqueeze
ai.onnx;14;Add,BatchNormalization,Mul,Relu,Reshape,Sub
com.microsoft;1;FusedConv,FusedMatMul,QuickGelu,SkipLayerNormalization
